  flourish, not a notifications system. So if the multi-user reading is right, the honest answer is a server, a DB, and
  auth. That's a real project, not a feature.

  [3mOn the sequencing.[0m Right now there are no tasks. A notifications system notifies about [3mchanges to tasks[0m, so building
  it first means inventing a task model, a change model, and a subscription model as a side effect of building the
  notification layer — three consequential decisions made as implementation details of the fourth. That's backwards, and
  those are exactly the decisions that get baked in wrong and are expensive to undo.

  [3mWhat notifications actually need from the rest of the app[0m is small and knowable: every task mutation goes through one
  chokepoint that emits a change event [38;5;153m{taskId, field, oldValue, newValue, actor, timestamp}[39m. That's the seam. If tasks
  are built with that chokepoint, adding notifications later — in-page, email, push, whatever — is additive. If tasks
  are built without it, you end up retrofitting change detection by diffing, which is where notification bugs live.

  So my recommendation is: [1mbuild tasks first, with that single change-event chokepoint, and treat notifications as the [0m
  [1mnext spec after it.[0m You get a working app sooner, and the notifications design gets made with real tasks in front of
  us instead of hypothetical ones.

  Two things I'd still want to pin down before designing anything, but they depend on which way you go here:
